import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';
---
<BlogLayout
  title="Bulk vs Small Batch: Sourcing Strategy for Apparel Brands"
  description="When to start with small batch sampling and when to scale to full production. A strategic guide for intimate apparel brands."
>
  <article class="container-site py-12 max-w-3xl">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a><span>/</span>
      <span class="text-navy font-medium">Bulk vs Small Batch</span>
    </nav>

    <time class="text-sm text-slate">May 18, 2026</time>
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-navy mt-2 mb-8">Bulk vs Small Batch: Sourcing Strategy for Apparel Brands</h1>

    <div class="prose prose-stone max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-stone leading-relaxed">
        One of the most common questions we hear from new brands is: should I start with small batches 
        or go straight to bulk production? The answer depends on where you are in your brand journey.
      </p>

      <h2>When to Choose Small Batch</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Testing the market:</strong> Validate demand for a new design before committing to large volumes</li>
        <li><strong>Evaluating quality:</strong> Run a short production batch to verify factory output consistency</li>
        <li><strong>Limited capital:</strong> Smaller cash commitment reduces financial risk</li>
        <li><strong>Pop-ups & pre-orders:</strong> Build hype around a limited release before scaling</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>When to Go Bulk</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Established demand:</strong> You have sales data showing consistent reordering</li>
        <li><strong>Lower unit cost:</strong> Bulk production reduces per-unit manufacturing and shipping costs by 15–30%</li>
        <li><strong>Retail distribution:</strong> Retailers and distributors require consistent inventory</li>
        <li><strong>Seasonal planning:</strong> Align bulk production with seasonal sales cycles</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Hybrid Approach</h2>
      <p>Many successful brands use a hybrid model: start with small batch sampling (through platforms like Viomisha), gather customer feedback, iterate the design, then scale to bulk production once product-market fit is validated.</p>

      <h2>Cost Comparison</h2>
      <p>While small batches have a higher per-unit cost (typically 20-40% above bulk pricing), they dramatically reduce the cost of mistakes. A bad design in bulk quantity can cost tens of thousands; a bad small batch costs hundreds.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-cream border-l-4 border-accent">
      <p class="text-sm text-navy font-semibold">Ready to scale from sample to bulk?</p>
      <p class="text-sm text-stone mt-1">Try small batches on <a href="https://viomisha.com" target="_blank" class="text-accent hover:underline">Viomisha</a>, then scale with us for full production. <a href="/contact" class="text-accent hover:underline">Get a bulk quote</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BlogLayout>
